\section{State the property for a changing program}

Xavier Leroy's \emph{Formal Verification of a Realistic Compiler} develops semantic preservation for CompCert: the reasoning concerns a source program and its translated program under specified semantics. An adaptive compiler adds transitions that create or retire versions while the program is already running. Showing that separately entered versions compute the right return value does not show that a live activation starts the right continuation or that installation cannot expose unfinished code.

For a small, deterministic guest language, require that a run with any permitted optimization policy yield the reference observation sequence: ordered guest emissions and memory-visible operations, followed by the same return value or exception, or nontermination as specified by the reference. Exclude profiling and compilation events from that language-level trace. Such exclusion alone does not permit the compiler to delay the guest forever. Use a progress argument that gives each finite run of compiler-only steps a bound, or state and prove a scheduling condition. An implementation that can continually requeue a failed job without executing the guest violates this part of the contract.

Aur\`ele Barri\`ere's \emph{Formal Verification of Just-in-Time Compilation} uses nested simulations: the outer simulation relates the executing JIT to the original program; its invariant includes an inner simulation relating the JIT's evolving program to the original. In the model, a bound on optimization steps prevents unbounded optimization-only stuttering. Magnus Myreen's \emph{Verified Just-In-Time Compiler on x86} shows a complementary low-level obligation by modeling execution of generated and modified machine code, including instruction-cache behavior, in HOL4. It treats both pre-generated and on-demand native code; that result does not certify an arbitrary speculative optimization or collector.

\section{Check a rewrite and its exit as separate cases}

Consider bytecode \texttt{emit(x); return exact\_neg(x)} where \(x\) is an arbitrary-precision signed integer. Exact negation does not change memory and never overflows. Under a small-integer specialization the value range is \([-2^{31},2^{31}-1]\).

\begin{verbatim}
ref P0: emit(x)
ref P1: return exact_neg(x)

opt:    emit(x)
        guard fits_i32(x) else deopt(P1, x)
        n, overflow = negate_checked_i32(unbox(x))
        guard not overflow else deopt(P1, x)
        return box_i32(n)

accept candidate only if:
  comparable observation prefix at both exits
  reconstructed x equals the original value at P1
  success implies boxed n equals exact_neg(x)
\end{verbatim}

The success property can be checked for the full finite machine domain with a bit-vector formula that relates sign-extended input and output to mathematical negation at a wider width. The guard's failure is not ``undefined behavior'': it must transfer to the reference before negation and after the one emission. Suppose a rewrite accidentally drops the overflow check. The value \(-2^{31}\) distinguishes the wrapped result \(-2^{31}\) from the correct \(2^{31}\). In another mutant replacing \texttt{P1} with \texttt{P0}, a forced exit emits twice even though the final result may be right. Test both mutants and force deoptimization even for a small ordinary value to exercise recovery separately from a real assumption failure (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-256}).

\Cref{fig:visual-258} isolates the two obligations of the optimized path: compute the exact result on success, and resume at the same observation boundary on failure.

