\subsection{Require Progress even under a Hostile Tier Policy}

An optimizer that always emits equivalent versions can still
prevent a guest from making progress by switching indefinitely.
For a small single-threaded proof model, let a scheduler choose
between a compiler-only transition and one semantic guest step.
A compiler-only transition may install code or change the
execution representation but must preserve the canonical guest
continuation and observation prefix. Count those transitions with
a fixed fuel budget per guest step. In this model each scheduled
transition itself terminates, and a nonterminal canonical state
always has an enabled guest step. This is a design for the proof
model, not a bound on a production compiler's wall time.

\begin{sudocode}[Bound stuttering between observations or guest work]
\State $left\gets B$
\While{guest is not terminal}
  \If{$left>0$ and policy requests an enabled compiler step}
    \State take one compiler-only step
      \Comment{Canonical state and observations unchanged}
    \State $left\gets left-1$
  \Else
    \State take one simulated semantic guest step
    \State $left\gets B$ \Comment{Reset only after guest progress}
  \EndIf
\EndWhile
\State \Return guest outcome
\end{sudocode}

Set $B=2$ and use a guest whose two steps are to emit 7
and return 11. Let the policy always request an enabled install.
An execution may perform two installs, emit 7, perform two
more installs, then return 11. A test that merely checks for
matching emissions would not distinguish this from a policy
that keeps installing forever before the first emission;
assert the step bound too. A mutant restoring $left$ on every
successful install fails that bound. With $B=0$ the same guest
observations occur with no installations.

The invariant combines canonical-state equivalence with a
strictly decreasing natural number across compiler-only steps.
An optimized machine operation that implements several guest
steps needs a separate simulation argument. A background job
also needs cancellation or scheduling guarantees: giving one
nonterminating job a finite number of modeled scheduler
tokens does not ensure anything about time to the next step.

\section{Accept, install and retire under different checks}

Dossier Chapter~03 explores speculation as IR, Chapter~29 proposes certified rewrite contracts, and Chapter~30 proposes transactional updates. Following the assurance plane of the blueprint, keep separate records of structural verification, refinement checking and runtime publication. SSA dominance of a type test is insufficient for a mutable heap fact: no intervening write or dependency change may invalidate it. A bounded validator that never models the frame map proves nothing about the cold exit. Record the semantics, coverage and bounds of every verdict; use the generic implementation for unsupported candidates if verified coverage is required. Dossier Chapter~39's semantic fingerprints can help find bugs but cannot close a proof gap.

Treat artifact publication as a new transition: check required dependency stamps at the commit point, make machine code and metadata visible together, and serialize revocation with entry. If running code depends on a fact about a changing environment, arrange a legal transfer before an operation may use the obsolete fact. Exercise the protocol by pausing a worker after validation, changing a dependency, and ensuring that its now-stale artifact cannot install. In separate tests revoke while a valid frame is live and check that the recovery metadata remains available. Local rewrites, frame transfers and publication preserve different invariants; a test or theorem about just one of them is not end-to-end assurance.
